own above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161988 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3b079fd-bbcc-5159-b897-a02296071f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161037} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fb0561-6a34-5c41-aed1-bd61e76a3743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161038} {:answer/id #uuid "0a3a1a82-5d2a-573d-b07f-dc28fdd6091b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161039} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc51304-8c87-5fe3-ab76-39024f9981f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161040} {:answer/id #uuid "597fa1a1-e9d1-5d71-a066-46e084ff79d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161041}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8a156a6b-3c25-5fa9-808b-9b0666d09d96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161036} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6632f3d7-06ae-5c79-b1d4-500c6c3802ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394951} {:answer/id #uuid "e1867d9b-476d-5a5d-89d7-ed8c8216d1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394952} {:answer/id #uuid "8c35dc64-6998-53e6-b3da-d3899a48d48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394953} {:answer/id #uuid "c19d5511-a039-5139-93ab-c5895b1de46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394954} {:answer/id #uuid "451d28cf-25fa-5618-a349-86bebd5f1545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "451d28cf-25fa-5618-a349-86bebd5f1545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394955} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff6651f3-29f1-57b9-8b08-9d44f2e66d1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dec4c6f4-86dd-50ba-b4f8-5dac075a34ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-94260" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161988 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97b892c9-66af-5f63-9cd5-08d6cd151877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394958} {:answer/id #uuid "976b0d9a-3e2a-5906-bea8-f65fa78ff984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394959} {:answer/id #uuid "afa81b49-a62b-5b06-85f4-00c25c2caf2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394960} {:answer/id #uuid "498c4572-93e7-5f84-afed-6a70d366ce31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394961} {:answer/id #uuid "03d7d1cc-f2ca-5cb0-aeef-81f728bc88ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afa81b49-a62b-5b06-85f4-00c25c2caf2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394960} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6916fa-9833-5f3f-887c-af297c3cc3e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f075746-4483-5586-942c-8cf99fac769f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94262" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=0.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=0.$ Computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161989 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b17109b-503d-5d39-8994-1c239e1d6f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355745} {:answer/id #uuid "8839af14-7b53-5d4e-bc41-0268ce08626d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355869} {:answer/id #uuid "acafb5b8-ec91-5d67-8ea0-125e56d52cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356192} {:answer/id #uuid "aecc46f1-2732-5322-92ae-9ab97cd8e884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356205} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a5541d-7944-5a33-9a5b-efd6cfaa8758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ \nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aecc46f1-2732-5322-92ae-9ab97cd8e884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356205} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7490596-2fa1-53bc-8ada-e942488fb1b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d443cf2-e450-59f7-912e-8007eb24ac35" :question/math-academy-id "q-94332" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are orthogonal. Finally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161989 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d33072-0e95-532d-bfe9-f8a1d3590433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364532} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca87cdf-7f9f-5145-ba0c-d03c91e9fb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364533} {:answer/id #uuid "fe282f17-8220-5c47-be34-dc1bd6837036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364534} {:answer/id #uuid "39533518-a289-5f09-a63f-224eb0e2e01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364535} {:answer/id #uuid "39851b58-e3a7-5559-9324-ebb962d9ce2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ca87cdf-7f9f-5145-ba0c-d03c91e9fb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364533} :answer-field/id #uuid "8040c4b6-73ba-50cc-80d0-f343c5c817c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc0d6469-4523-5a6a-b241-3d642027e56c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94345" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161989 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "274efcfe-f893-5105-aa2f-fd156c58b475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354997} {:answer/id #uuid "b46ab4aa-3d20-53f3-bd11-038693dbf688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356249} {:answer/id #uuid "d310ec2c-fbb3-5ef7-ba9a-fc3cd195ad9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356505} {:answer/id #uuid "d77a84a6-94a5-5209-b6fd-977d16bd294a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356549} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa9e02e-3860-50bc-b5d0-8cd92dc992c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d310ec2c-fbb3-5ef7-ba9a-fc3cd195ad9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356505} :answer-field/id #uuid "dad9f5dc-8923-584b-b5c4-0c96615d0676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fecab0a-cd00-5e03-af2a-81daa57c4cbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-94351" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second and third columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{2}={v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & ={v}_{3}-\\frac{{v}_{3}\\cdot {e}_{2}}{{e}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{2}}{e}_{2} \\\\  & ={v}_{3}-\\frac{1}{5}{e}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{2}$ and ${e}_{3}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the second and third columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{3}}{‖{e}_{3}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{24}{5}}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
